\begin_layout Title
Structural vs.
 Reduced Form Approaches to Empirical Research
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Introduction
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Internal Validity 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The degree to which the research design yields estimates that reflect the
 true casual effect for the population, time, and/or location under investigatio
n.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Threats
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Functional form (linearity, interactions, etc.)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Omitted variable bias, endogeneity, selection bias
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Measurement error in regressors
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
External Validity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The degree to which the research design yields estimates that reflect the
 true casual effect for other population, times, and/or locations of interest
 that lie outside of the population, times, and/or locations that were studied.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider the model for individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 from group 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, location 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, and time 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{iplt}=\alpha+\left(\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{iplt}\right)T_{iplt}+\omega_{iplt}\theta+\epsilon_{iplt}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{iplt}$
\end_inset

 are characteristics that relate to the treatment effect (
\series bold
\emph on
interactive confounders
\series default
\emph default
), and 
\begin_inset Formula $\omega_{iplt}$
\end_inset

 are characteristics that affect the outcome directly (
\series bold
\emph on
additive confounders
\series default
\emph default
).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Threats
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Lack of internal validity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Interaction of setting and treatment
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Consider an experiment in which 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{iplt}$
\end_inset

 is randomly assigned (RA), then our estimate will be internally valid.
 The estimate will reflect the average treatment effect for the distribution
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $x$
\end_inset

 that is involved in the experiment.
 Whether the estimates are externally valid depends on:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
the extent to which the population of interest is reflected in the study.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
the degree to which treatment effects are heterogeneous across 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $l$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
internal validity.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Section*
Structural Form vs Reduced Form
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The battleground:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The credibility of theoretical vs statistical assumptions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Structural econometricians tend to make more theoretical assumptions, while
 reduced from econometricians tend to make more statistical assumptions.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Achieving external validity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The types of questions that can and should be asked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Structural Approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It was developed to bridge the gap between theory and empirics.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Theory is often used as a starting point, which allows for interdependencies
 among outcomes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Specifies cause and relationships 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Some variables are endogenous, which are determined within the model, while
 others are exogenous
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Equations are generated that correspond to individual choices and/or aggregates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The data is used to estimate parameters.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Maximum likelihood estimation of non-linear simultaneous equations is quite
 common.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The parameters present in the model are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fundamental
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 in that they are arguably not context dependent (e.g.
 risk aversion, preferences, technology).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
These primitives allow for generalizability, i.e., out of sample prediction.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
We can simulate behavior responses to policies that haven't happened.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We can conduct welfare calculations
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Weaknesses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Identification: there is usually weak support for the exogeneity assumptions
 and/or instruments.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The theoretical assumptions may be overly restrictive, but this is hard
 to assess.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Due to convergence at local extrema, estimates can be highly sensitive to
 starting values (Knittel & Meta, RESTAT).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Reduced Form Approach
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
This method is also known as the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

design-based
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 or the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

quasi-experimental
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 approach.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
It doesn't try to uncover the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

true model
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 that is generating the data.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Its primary focus is on research design.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
The main objective is to obtain credible causal estimates of the parameters
 of interest.
 Note that these are not necessarily going to be 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

fundamental
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 parameters.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Reduced form econometricians see actual experiments as the gold standard
 since they are intuitive and straight forward.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The conceptual framework highlights the source of variation.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
If experiments are not possible, then we must search for circumstances where
 it is reasonable to assume that unobservable variables are unrelated to
 the variable of interest.
 These circumstances may be driven by chance, forces of nature, or institutions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Since the variation is clearly articulated, then we know what threats need
 to be examined, i.e., we can make a data driven case that we have exogeneity.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsubsection*
Weaknesses, Criticisms, Responses
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
External validity: if there is substantial heterogeneity, then this is a
 very serious issue.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
The reduced form approach is divorced from theory.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
It can't speak for mechanisms, which are often examined empirically for
 plausability.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Economic theory guides interpretation of the evidence and the questions
 that are asked.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Studies often try to test competing theoretical models which offer different
 predictions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

In a design based framework, economic theory helps us understand the picture
 that emerges from a constellation of empirical findings, but does not help
 us paint the picture.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset


\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
Major data requirements: if the data does not exist, then we cannot procede.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Difficult to say anything conclusive about welfare, efficiency, or behavioral
 responses to policies that haven't occured.
 
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize
It does, however, tell us which policy levers we can pull to get the types
 of results that we want to see.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
We still have to worry about unintended consequences with respect to outcomes
 that haven't been thoroughly investigated.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Similar issues are arise for variables that are 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

outside the model
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 of structural work.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Subsection*
Clarification
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Structural approaches involve modeling the system in which a collection
 of endogenous variables are simultaneously determined by one another, by
 exogenous variables, and of course, by agents maximizing utility and/or
 profits.
 The researcher makes several choices here.
 What to specify as endogenously determined vs exogenously determined? What
 functional forms to use? The internal validity of the estimates depend
 on these theoretical assumptions.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Reduced form approaches don't involve such choices.
 They "bypass estimation of the underlying economic model" and instead focus
 on the "reduced form" effects of variables of interest that are arguably
 exogenous in the circumstances under consideration.
 The internal validity of the estimates depends on whether a variable of
 interest is truly uncorrelated with other variables that affect outcomes
 in this set of circumstances.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Example*
Suppose we wish to examine the effect of class size on test scores.
 The two approaches provide different outlooks on how to proceed.
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Reduced form: 
\series default
With this approach, we must document the effect of class size reduction.
 The reduced form method starts with the parameter of interest and usually
 employs a linear function.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y=\alpha+\beta\ \mbox{class size}+\epsilon.
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Structural:
\series default
 With this approach, we create a model with several equations and variables,
 and we specify the interactions between them.
 In this example, there is a relationship between class size and the following
 variables: teacher quality, teacher effort, parental effort, and student
 effort.
 The structural approach models all of these interactions, and the listed
 variables all affect test scores.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
